#: ../../source/docs/configuration-files-for-common-airframes.rst:5
#: fc67cd835c2143119f747b022c5866c5
msgid "Configuration Values for Common Airframes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/configuration-files-for-common-airframes.rst:7
#: 0e424ad1b23a409c825537ed65acc1fd
msgid "This page provides some approximate configuration values for common airframes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/configuration-files-for-common-airframes.rst:12
#: 44d0d58ad0394624a8740d9661ae650f
msgid "These values are good enough to “get you off the ground”. For optimum performance you will still need to tune these parameters for your aircraft."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/configuration-files-for-common-airframes.rst:17
#: babdf7a287404e148f01a913b2a4c338
msgid "Configuration values - not complete files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/configuration-files-for-common-airframes.rst:19
#: e18108793e9a4ece84519497ddb1fe11
msgid "We do not not provide complete configuration files because we do not want to encourage sharing complete configuration files between APM users."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/configuration-files-for-common-airframes.rst:23
#: f444783bd18b4c60835828633bdd87e5
msgid "Sharing configuration files is often a bad idea because the correct settings for a specific plane are dependent on a lot more factors than just what base airframe type is being used:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/configuration-files-for-common-airframes.rst:27
#: aac5600edd9148969d4f593971bcc1ed
msgid "the right tuning values depends on what type of servos you have installed (different servos respond at different speeds), and which hole in the servo horns you connect the control arms to"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/configuration-files-for-common-airframes.rst:30
#: e22757a56bbf4be488100d569113d25d
msgid "the right values also depend on your RC transmitter setup, especially the range of movement of each control axis"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/configuration-files-for-common-airframes.rst:32
#: eb06f078249b4215a6348cc30c2f81e2
msgid "the right tuning values also depend on what motor and battery you have installed, and depends a lot on the total takeoff weight of your aircraft. If one user has a camera installed and another user doesn't then they will probably need different tuning values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/configuration-files-for-common-airframes.rst:36
#: 282d9a2d244844aab2ec5c5640a9ad08
msgid "manufacturers often make changes to airframes, servos and motors without re-branding the airframe as a new model. Sometimes they even change the direction that servos move"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/configuration-files-for-common-airframes.rst:39
#: 231516d8ea304bc4910a83efbf5c95d9
msgid "different users have different optional sensors, which changes what configuration options are needed. For example, some users install an airspeed sensor or an external compass."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/configuration-files-for-common-airframes.rst:42
#: 3c54d6862c164e69b4b0b4dbcd81e453
msgid "new Ardupilot firmware releases frequently change the defaults for some critical parameters to avoid problems found by users. If you load a config file from a user of an older firmware then that will revert those changes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/configuration-files-for-common-airframes.rst:47
#: b211c80fa2614dc2a08f66c5e429e07e
msgid "For all of these reasons you should not just download a configuration file from another user and expect it to work."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/configuration-files-for-common-airframes.rst:51
#: 82a54074976f439ba8713f8703a18006
msgid "So what can you do?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/configuration-files-for-common-airframes.rst:53
#: f5da3b46ea1f4d4c92007554304961c9
msgid "If another user has tuned a similar aircraft, then you can look in their configuration file to find the key tuning parameter they used, and manually put those values into your APM as a starting point for tuning. The key parameters you should look for are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/configuration-files-for-common-airframes.rst:58
#: 3b9f5b789f464dfa9edf0032d668c5bc
msgid "``RLL2SRV_P``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/configuration-files-for-common-airframes.rst:59
#: 1cdb317774e441328edd49bfdd9ba322
msgid "``PTCH2SRV_P``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/configuration-files-for-common-airframes.rst:60
#: 8259ef3ea3d84e7a94e2b4062423e670
msgid ":ref:`NAVL1_PERIOD <NAVL1_PERIOD>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/configuration-files-for-common-airframes.rst:62
#: 7bb8468ab1d04bbebfec2d2adacb99cf
msgid "Setting those to an approximately correct value will give you a reasonable starting point for tuning your airframe. After that you should follow the :ref:`tuning guide <roll-pitch-controller-tuning>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/configuration-files-for-common-airframes.rst:66
#: 91824351b5094460b4cd60759a4f43ef
msgid "Also note that the Plane parameters default to zero 'I' values. The reason for this is that you need to tune the P value first, then tune the I value. If you put in a non-zero I value when the P value is a long way off the aircraft may oscillate badly and could crash."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/configuration-files-for-common-airframes.rst:71
#: dc3d949042eb4acb96063cd4c407dac5
msgid "Once you have good P values then you should raise the I values a bit, as described in the tuning guide. That will allow APM to cope better with wind. For example, once the P values are setup correctly then setting the following will help with most aircraft:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/configuration-files-for-common-airframes.rst:76
#: b143e0a4c81d466c94baff0d2e22dd81
msgid "``RLL2SRV_I`` : 0.05"
msgstr ""

